  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n - Thunderstorms return to Lemhi County, Idaho, and southwest\n   Montana this weekend, bringing lightning and brief, erratic\n   wind gusts. \n\n - A minor cooling trend through Monday will be followed by a\n   warming trend through mid-week.\n \n\nA surge of monsoonal moisture currently visible on water vapor \nsatellite imagery over northern California and southern Oregon \nwill filter into the Northern Rockies this evening. As this \nmoisture advances, mid- to high-level cloud cover will gradually \noverspread the region from the south tonight.\n\nBy Saturday, this moisture plume will interact with a weak \nshortwave trough dropping out of the northwest. This interaction \nwill provide enough lift and instability to trigger scattered \nthunderstorms, primarily focused across Lemhi County, Idaho, and \nsouthwest Montana. While moisture levels are not exceptional, they\nare sufficient to fuel storms capable of producing cloud-to-\nground lightning and brief, gusty outflow winds. Outdoor \nrecreationists in these areas should monitor the sky and be \nprepared to seek shelter. For Saturday's thunderstorm potential, \nconfidence is high that storms will initiate in southwest Montana\nand Lemhi County. However, there is moderate uncertainty \nregarding the northern extent of this convection. Roughly 20% of \nensemble members suggest the shortwave could dig slightly further \nwest, which would pull scattered showers as far north as the \nMissoula or Bitterroot valleys. \n\nThe passage of this weak trough will slightly depress \ntemperatures across the region through Monday, keeping daytime \nhighs comfortable and close to seasonal averages. However, as the \nshortwave exits, ensemble guidance is in strong agreement that a \nprominent upper-level ridge along the US West Coast will \nstrengthen and expand eastward. This building ridge will kick off \na warming and drying trend across central Idaho and western \nMontana starting Tuesday. Strong subsidence under the high \npressure will lead to clear skies and lighter winds. High \ntemperatures will climb into the upper 80s to near 90 degrees in \nwestern Montana valleys by Wednesday, marking some of the hottest \ndays of the year so far. Lower elevation river valleys in central \nIdaho will likely push into the mid-to-upper 90s.\n\nUncertainty emerges by late next week, where about 50% of the \nensemble model clusters depict a deeper trough approaching the \nPacific Northwest by Friday. If this deeper trough materializes, \nit would bring breezier conditions and increased shower chances to\nthe region slightly earlier than currently forecast.",
